# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 1: General Description
#
# The fourth picture theme, and the first EVENING / HOLIDAY scene: a Luxembourg
# City Christmas market food stall at dusk, lit up with strings of lights and
# garlands. Where the other three photos ask "what season, what time of day", this
# one already answers both visually — so its new inference angle is different: is
# this a normal day or a special one, and how busy is it. That is drilled in 03.
#
# The photo: a wooden Christmas-market stall selling grilled food, decorated with
# fir garlands, red baubles and strings of warm lights, with a large model
# hamburger, a reindeer figure and a red Santa figure on its roof. Two men stand at
# the counter ordering; several children in winter coats and one in a high-vis
# vest stand in the foreground; more stalls and bare trees with fairy lights are
# visible behind, and the sky is grey daytime overcast rather than dark.
#
# The photo's coverage is split across three sub-lessons, one per exam task:
#   01 — WOU + WAT: naming the place, the objects and their positions, and what
#        the people are doing.                              ← this file
#   02 — WIEN: the people, 1–2 of them described visually.
#   03 — WÉINI: the time of day and whether this looks like an ordinary day.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION. No @question examiner prompts and no first-person opinion,
# preference or feeling — those belong to the topic themes. A depicted person's
# visible state is fine: that is observation. Rule of thumb — if a stranger could
# not check the sentence against the photo, it does not belong here.
#
# Not authored, for failing exactly that test: the stall's own signage
# ("Grill Bar Rond", "Choucroute Garnie") and prices on the chalkboard. A brand
# name or a price tag is neither Luxembourgish vocabulary nor checkable
# description of the *scene*, so the stall ships here as the plain
# `de Stand mat Iessen`.
#
# Vocabulary and grammar are held to A1–A2 deliberately, same as the other
# picture themes: present tense, simple main clauses, spatial adverbs +
# prepositions, no attributive adjectives on masculine or neuter nouns.
#
# This file NAMES THE PLACE, which is the examiner's first question:
# de Chrëschtmaart. LOD lists it as a plain dictionary compound ("Christmas
# market"), so it is safe to teach as ordinary vocabulary, not a proper noun.
#
# Nouns are taught in the number the content actually uses (d'Luuchten,
# d'Kanner, d'Buden as plurals) — an unused singular is a free Element standing
# between the learner and the 100% gate.
#
# Ten @fill blocks, not the usual three — this is the theme's answering-pattern
# drill, so it carries more of the reusable frame library than a single-photo
# file would otherwise need: the existential opener, the background/foreground
# pair, the two-coordinated-nouns inventory, the exemplifying `Zum Beispill`
# listing, the additive `Ausserdeem` listing, the place-noun frame, and the
# what-are-they-doing frame — most reused shapes from the library, each given a
# pairing or blank no earlier picture theme has taught. See the skill's
# references/content-contract.md for the mechanized bounds.

@lesson P3.01 "Chrëschtmaart: General Description"

# Pre-cropped to 16:9 and encoded as WebP at 880px — see
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md for the pipeline. @image-alt stays:
# it is the a11y text, and the caption of the placeholder frame if the file is
# ever missing.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/chreschtmaart/img/chreschtmaart.webp"
@image-alt "A decorated Christmas-market food stall at a city square: strings of warm lights and fir garlands with red baubles, a large model hamburger and a reindeer figure on the roof, two men ordering at the counter, several children in winter coats in the foreground, more stalls and bare trees with lights behind."

# --- Talking about a picture ---
@word d'Bild = the picture
@word gesinn = to see

# --- Naming the place ---
@word de Chrëschtmaart = the Christmas market
@word d'Stad = the city
@word d'Plaz = the square

# --- What is at the market ---
@word de Stand = the stall
@word d'Buden = the stalls
@word d'Iessen = the food
@word d'Luuchten = the lights
@word d'Guirlande = the garland
@word de Bam = the tree
@word d'Beem = the trees

# --- Positions ---
@word lénks = on the left
@word riets = on the right
@word virun = in front of
@word de Vierdergrond = the foreground
@word den Hannergrond = the background

# --- People and what they do ---
@word d'Leit = the people
@word d'Kanner = the children
@word bestellen = to order
@word waarden = to wait
@word verkafen = to sell
@word d'Famill = the family
@word d'Famillen = the families
@word d'Fotoen = the photos

@sentence
@lu Dëst ass e Bild vum Chrëschtmaart.
@en This is a picture of the Christmas market.
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-lu Schoul
@distractor-en shop
@distractor-en school

@sentence
@lu Et si vill Leit op der Plaz.
@en There are a lot of people on the square.
@distractor-lu keng
@distractor-lu Buden
@distractor-en no
@distractor-en stalls

@sentence
@lu De Chrëschtmaart ass an der Stad.
@en The Christmas market is in the city.
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Haus
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en house

@sentence
@lu Zwee Männer bestellen um Stand.
@en Two men are ordering at the stall.
@distractor-lu waarden
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-en waiting
@distractor-en children

@sentence
@lu Virun dem Stand stinn e puer Kanner.
@en In front of the stall there are a few children.
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu Männer
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en men

@sentence
@lu De Stand ass mat Luuchten a Guirlande dekoréiert.
@en The stall is decorated with lights and a garland.
@distractor-lu Blieder
@distractor-lu Wolleken
@distractor-en leaves
@distractor-en clouds

@sentence
@lu Hannert dem Stand gesinn ech méi Buden a Beem.
@en Behind the stall I see more stalls and trees.
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-en in front
@distractor-en children

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The inventory frame with two coordinated nouns — the shape Schueberfouer and
# groussgaass already used, but with a pairing neither has taught. Only the
# second side is a blank (both sides blanked would violate R6 by construction),
# and `d'` stays fixed in the frame, so masculine `Bam` and `Chrëschtmaart` are
# both ruled out on grammar as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn d'Buden an d'[Luuchten].
@en I see the stalls and the [lights].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Chrëschtmaart
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en Christmas market

# The exemplifying-listing frame — `Zum Beispill` introduces one example from a
# larger scene, a pattern that transfers to any busy photo. `e puer` forces a
# plural, so the singular distractors fail on agreement as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Zum Beispill gesinn ech e puer [Kanner].
@en For example I see a few [children].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Stand
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en stall

# The background inventory — the same frame groussgaass used for buildings, here
# applied to a scene word neither picture theme has taught yet. `méi héich` is
# fixed, so the blank is a plural noun; both distractors are singular and fail
# agreement as well as the photo.
@fill
@lu Am Hannergrond ginn et méi héich [Beem].
@en In the background there are more tall [trees].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Stand
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en stall

# The existential opener Schueberfouer used for a busy funfair, reused here for a
# busy market — the idiom `vill lass` transfers to any crowded scene, which is
# what makes it a frame rather than a sentence about this photo. Both
# distractors describe the opposite scene, so neither works in the blank.
@fill
@lu Um Chrëschtmaart ass vill [lass].
@en At the Christmas market a lot is [happening].
@distractor-lu roueg
@distractor-lu zou
@distractor-en quiet
@distractor-en closed

# The background/foreground pair, mirrored from Schueberfouer with new nouns:
# the position word stays fixed in the frame and the scene noun is the blank, so
# `d'` rules out masculine tiles on grammar (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Am Vierdergrond gesinn ech d'[Kanner].
@en In the foreground I see the [children].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Stand
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en stall

# The additive-listing frame groussgaass used for street furniture, reused here
# for the market's food and photos. `Ausserdeem` forces inversion (`gesinn ech`),
# an order the learner reads rather than assembles. Two blanks of different
# number and slot (plural noun, then singular after the fixed `d'`), so they
# cannot swap; `d'` also keeps both blanks clear of the Eifeler Regel (R5),
# unlike an indefinite `en` would.
@fill
@lu Ausserdeem gesinn ech [Fotoen] an d'[Iessen].
@en Besides I see [photos] and the [food].
@distractor-lu Buden
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en stalls
@distractor-en tree

# The place-noun frame from groussgaass, reused for a different named place.
# `an der` stays fixed, so the blank takes only a feminine noun — ruling out
# masculine `Chrëschtmaart` and `Bam` on grammar alone (R4) as well as on the
# photo.
@fill
@lu D'Leit op dem Bild sinn an enger [Stad].
@en The people in the picture are in a [city].
@distractor-lu Chrëschtmaart
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en Christmas market
@distractor-en tree

# The what-are-they-doing frame from living-room, reused with a new verb. The
# blank is the verb, and the plural subject `d'Leit` makes the singular form the
# sharpest distractor — a noun cannot occupy a verb slot at all (R3).
@fill
@lu Um Chrëschtmaart [kucken] d'Leit d'Buden.
@en At the Christmas market the people [look at] the stalls.
@distractor-lu kuckt
@distractor-lu Iesswueren
@distractor-en looks at
@distractor-en food

# The exemplifying frame reused with the market's families, and a second blank
# in a different clause and word class (place noun after the fixed `um`), so
# they cannot swap (R6).
@fill
@lu Zum Beispill gesinn ech e puer [Famillen] um [Chrëschtmaart].
@en For example I see a few [families] at the [Christmas market].
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-en children
@distractor-en shop

# The Schueberfouer foreground/background mirror frame, here with the position
# word as the blank instead of the noun — the pair the examiner listens for.
# Both distractors are grammatical in the slot and factually wrong about the
# photo (the family stands in front of the stall, not behind it).
@fill
@lu Am [Vierdergrond] gesinn ech eng Famill.
@en In the [foreground] I see a family.
@distractor-lu Hannergrond
@distractor-lu Chrëschtmaart
@distractor-en background
@distractor-en Christmas market
